 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity**: Billboard generation is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of billboards. Sorting is $O(N^2)$ due to the selection sort implementation in the source [S1, unofficial]. GPU upload is $O(N)$. Rendering is $O(N)$ draw calls (or 1 instanced draw call).
+-   **Time Complexity**: Billboard generation is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of billboards. Sorting is $O(N^2)$ due to the selection sort implementation in the source [S1, unofficial]. GPU upload is $O(N)$. Rendering is $O(N)$ draw calls (or 1 instanced draw call) [S1, unofficial].
 -   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for storing positions and scales on CPU and GPU [S1, unofficial].
--   **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
--   **Scaling**: Performance degrades significantly with large $N$ due to the $O(N^2)$ sorting step. The method is suitable for moderate numbers of billboards.
--   **Strengths**: Simple implementation using standard OpenGL features; leverages instancing for efficient rendering; CPU-side sorting allows for depth-based rendering order.
--   **Limitations**: $O(N^2)$ sorting is inefficient for large $N$; relies on CPU for generation and sorting; does not handle occlusion culling efficiently.
--   **When to use**: Small to medium-sized volumes where simplicity is preferred and $N$ is manageable.
--   **When not to use**: Large-scale volumes requiring real-time performance with high $N$, or when GPU-side processing is available and preferred.
